STATUS MARKERS
 ✅            Done. Evidence in the note, the PR, or both.
 🛑 [BLOCKED]  Cannot proceed. An external dependency is missing; the note says which.
 🚫 [DEFERRED] Deliberately not doing this. A decision was taken; the note says why.
 (PENDING)     Work is done but unconfirmed — waiting on a run, a check, or an observation.
 (NOT DONE)    Not done, not blocked. Still owed.
 (unmarked)    Not started.
A step is only ✅ when its own How: column was actually carried out. "Mostly done" is not ✅ —
it is PENDING or NOT DONE with a note, so the gap stays visible instead of dissolving.

PHASE I — 30% → 50%
Block A — Canonical data model
 ✅ [SPEC] Write /docs/features/data_model.md with all five tables, columns, types, keys, indexes
How: Give the architect the current SQLite schema plus the target from the roadmap; require explicit types and constraints.
Why: Everything downstream speaks indicator × geography × period × value. Getting it wrong now means rewriting every adapter later.
 ✅ [SPEC] Define the observations primary key as (indicator_id, geo_id, period, vintage)
How: State it in the spec and in the migration as a real constraint, not a convention.
Why: Without vintage in the key, a revised government figure overwrites history and your revision analysis becomes impossible. Without the constraint, duplicate rows silently double your averages.
 ✅ [SPEC] Define period formats and their sorting rules
How: One period string column plus frequency; document that 2024-Q3 sorts lexically and 2024-7 is invalid.
Why: Mixed annual, quarterly and monthly data in one table is where date bugs live. Zero-padding decided late means re-writing millions of rows.
 ✅ [SPEC] Define the status enum
How: final | provisional | estimate | revised | suppressed | na, documented with which source uses which.
Why: Statbel suppresses small-cell values. If suppression looks like zero, you will publish "median income €0" for a small commune.
 ✅ [SPEC] Define preferred_direction
How: Enum on the indicator config: lower_is_better | higher_is_better | neutral | contextual.
Why: Every later feature (arrows, percentiles, scores, commentary) needs to know whether high is good. Encoding it once prevents the classic "unemployment is in the 95th percentile 🎉" bug.
 ✅ [REVIEW] Review the model against mergers, sector data, source conflicts, revisions
How: Give the reviewer the spec and ask specifically: what breaks when two sources publish different values for the same cell?
Why: These four scenarios are the ones that force schema changes. Better to discover them in a text file than in a migration on 2 million rows.
 ✅ [H] Approve and freeze; later changes need an ADR
How: Comment "approved" on the issue; add docs/decisions/0001-data-model.md.
Why: Frozen means agents stop proposing improvements to it every session, which is otherwise a permanent tax on your attention.
 ✅ [BUILD] Create the target folder structure
How: src/{fetchers,transforms,validation,analytics,exporters}, config/{indicators,sources,geography}, data/{raw,processed,exports}, tests/.
Why: Structure is instruction. Given a fetchers/ folder, an agent puts a fetcher there; given a flat repo, it appends 300 lines to belgian_macro_db.py.
 ✅ [BUILD] Add a migration system
How: Numbered .sql files plus a tiny runner tracking applied versions in a schema_migrations table. No ORM.
Why: You need to reproduce any past schema to reproduce any past report. An ORM adds a large dependency and hides exactly the SQL you need to review.
 ✅ [BUILD] Implement 001_core_schema.sql
How: Straight translation of the approved spec, with FKs enabled (PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON).
Why: SQLite silently ignores foreign keys unless you turn them on, which means integrity constraints you believe exist do not.
 ✅ [BUILD] Implement 002_indexes.sql
How: At minimum observations(geo_id, indicator_id) and observations(indicator_id, period).
Why: At 581 communes × 200 indicators × 20 years you are near 2.5 million rows. Unindexed, the export step goes from seconds to many minutes inside a GitHub Actions time budget.
 ✅ [BUILD] Port the 17 existing macro indicators into the new model with geo_id = BE
How: A one-off script reading the old table, mapping to new ids, writing with vintage set to the migration date.
Why: Proves the new model can hold the old data. If national data does not fit, municipal data certainly will not.
 ✅ [BUILD] Tests: round-trip, PK collision, FK violation, migration idempotency
How: pytest with a temp SQLite file per test.
Why: Idempotency in particular: the daily workflow re-runs migrations every day. A non-idempotent migration breaks production once and then every day after.
 ✅ [RED] Independent audit of schema and migration
How: Hand over only the SQL and the spec, not the discussion.
Why: Schema errors are cheap now and catastrophic after 200 indicators depend on them.
 ✅ [H] Run the migration on a copy and eyeball 20 rows against the old CSV
How: Copy the DB, run, then compare specific GDP_QUARTERLY_YY values by hand.
Why: Automated tests confirm the code does what it says. Only your eyes confirm it does what you meant.
